# Git and pull requests

## Commits

- **English only** — commit messages, PR titles, and review notes.
- Imperative subject line ≤ 72 chars describing the *why-level* change
  (`fix: prevent duplicate refresh on concurrent 401s`), optional body for context.
- Conventional-commit prefixes (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `chore:`)
  keep history machine-readable.
- Every commit on a shared branch passes the quality gates
  (`workflow/01-quality-gates.mdc`).
- Never commit secrets, env files with real values, or signing material
  (`security/07-supply-chain-secrets.mdc`). A leaked secret is rotated, not just removed.

## Branches

- Short-lived feature branches off the main branch; rebase or merge from main frequently
  to keep diffs reviewable.
- Branch names: `feat/<domain>-<summary>`, `fix/<domain>-<summary>`.

## Pull requests

- **Single coherent intent per PR** — no unrelated refactors mixed into feature work.
  Large mechanical changes (renames, formatting) ship as separate PRs.
- Description answers three questions: **what** changed, **why**, and **how it was
  verified** (commands run, screens/stories exercised, platforms checked).
- Screenshots or Storybook links for visible changes.
- New dependencies and non-trivial performance costs are called out explicitly.
- Security-review triggers (`security/01-security-principles.mdc`) are declared when hit.
- The author runs all quality gates locally **before** requesting review.

## Review blockers (non-exhaustive)

- Missing tests for new rules modules or changed decision logic.
- Ad-hoc UI bypassing the design system; hard-coded copy or style values.
- Accessibility gaps on interactive surfaces.
- TypeScript/lint failures or forbidden suppressions.
- Sensitive data in the wrong storage tier; unvalidated external input.
- Stale or missing documentation for new public surfaces.
